/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Space and Cosmos (y batch) */
WB.L("auroras-space-weather", "space", "beginner", "Auroras and space weather",
  "The southern lights are a solar storm hitting Earth's invisible shield, and the same storms can knock out power grids and drag satellites from the sky.",
  [
    ["Light from the Sun", "Auroras are light shows high in the atmosphere, triggered by the solar wind streaming from the Sun.",
      "On the night of 10 May 2024, people across southern Australia stepped outside to find the sky glowing pink and green. The aurora australis, or southern lights, was seen far further north than usual during one of the strongest geomagnetic storms in about two decades. Auroras usually hang around the polar regions, in curtains, arcs and rays roughly 100 to several hundred kilometres up. Their northern twin is the aurora borealis.\n\nBoth begin at the Sun. Its corona, over a million degrees, boils into space as the solar wind, charged particles streaming outward at hundreds of kilometres per second and usually reaching Earth in a few days. Solar activity rises and falls over roughly 11 years. The latest maximum, around 2024 to 2025, is why so many Australians saw auroras."],
    ["Earth's invisible shield", "Earth's magnetic field deflects most of the solar wind and funnels some particles towards the poles.",
      "Earth is protected by a magnetic bubble, the magnetosphere, generated by molten iron churning in the outer core. The solar wind squashes it on the Sun-facing side and stretches it into a long tail on the other. Most of the solar wind flows around it. But magnetic field lines dip into the atmosphere near the magnetic poles, and energy stored in the magnetosphere can drive charged particles down along them. That is why auroras form in rings, called auroral ovals, around both magnetic poles. When the solar wind is calm, the southern oval sits over Antarctica and the Southern Ocean. When the Sun hits hard, the ovals expand towards the equator, and Tasmania and the mainland get a show."],
    ["Why the colours differ", "Different gases at different heights glow different colours when struck by incoming particles.",
      "An aurora is a little like a neon sign. Fast particles slam into atoms and molecules of air, giving them energy, which they release as light. The colour depends on which gas is hit and how high up it is. Oxygen around 100 to 250 kilometres up gives the most common colour, a vivid green. Higher still, where the air is extremely thin, oxygen can glow deep red, which is why great storms often paint the top of the sky crimson. Nitrogen adds blues and purples, often along the lower edges. Our eyes are poor at colour in dim light, so a faint aurora can look pale grey. A camera gathering light for several seconds often reveals colours you couldn't see."],
    ["When the Sun storms", "Solar flares and coronal mass ejections can cause geomagnetic storms that disrupt power, satellites, radio and navigation.",
      "When tangled magnetic fields near sunspots snap and reconnect, they release enormous energy. A solar flare is an intense flash of radiation reaching Earth in about eight minutes. A coronal mass ejection (CME) is a vast cloud of magnetised gas, billions of tonnes of it, usually arriving one to three days later. If its magnetic field lines up the right way, it can spark a geomagnetic storm. The largest on record, the Carrington Event of 1859, sent auroras far into the tropics and made telegraph systems spark and fail.\n\nIn March 1989 a storm induced currents in Quebec's power grid, and millions lost electricity for hours. In February 2022, SpaceX lost dozens of new Starlink satellites after a modest storm increased drag on them. Storms can also scramble radio and GPS."],
    ["Chasing the southern lights", "Your best chance in Australia is a dark, clear, moonless night with a low southern horizon during a strong storm.",
      "Tasmania is Australia's aurora capital, but strong storms can bring displays to Victoria, South Australia, southern New South Wales and southern Western Australia. Get well away from city lights, ideally somewhere with a clear, low southern horizon such as a beach or hilltop. A cloudless, moonless night helps enormously. Auroras can appear at any time of year, often around the middle of the night, and can surge and fade within minutes, so patience pays.\n\nSign up for alerts from the Bureau of Meteorology's space weather service, which also watches the Sun and issues warnings, or follow local aurora groups online. Bring warm clothes and a camera set to night mode or long exposure. Even a faint glow can photograph in vivid colour."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the solar wind?", ["Air currents in Earth's upper atmosphere","Light reflected off the Moon","Gas escaping from Earth into space","A stream of charged particles flowing out from the Sun"], 3, "The Sun's hot corona constantly leaks charged particles that flow outward through the solar system."],
    [1, "Why do auroras mostly appear near the poles?", ["The cold air there makes gases glow brightly","The magnetic field steers particles to the poles","The Sun only shines on the polar regions","There is much more oxygen at the poles"], 1, "Magnetic field lines dip into the atmosphere near the magnetic poles, so auroras form in ovals around them."],
    [2, "What usually produces the most common green colour in auroras?", ["Nitrogen near the ground","Water vapour in clouds","Oxygen roughly 100 to 250 kilometres up","Carbon dioxide"], 2, "Energised oxygen atoms at those heights release green light."],
    [3, "Why did SpaceX lose dozens of Starlink satellites in February 2022?", ["A geomagnetic storm increased their drag","They were struck by a passing meteor shower","They ran out of fuel before reaching orbit","They collided with other satellites in orbit"], 0, "Storms heat and expand the upper atmosphere, dragging down satellites in very low orbits."],
    [4, "What gives the best chance of seeing the southern lights?", ["A well-lit city rooftop just after sunset","A dark, moonless night with a clear southern view","A cloudy night at a busy, well-lit lookout","A bright full-moon night facing north"], 1, "Dark skies, a clear southern view and a strong storm give the best odds."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A solar storm lit Australian skies in 2024. The same storms can pull satellites down."});

WB.L("meteors-meteorites", "space", "beginner", "Shooting stars and meteorites",
  "Most shooting stars are specks of dust smaller than a grain of rice, yet a few space rocks survive the fall and carry chemistry older than the Sun.",
  [
    ["Not really a star", "A shooting star is a speck of space rock or dust burning up in the atmosphere, and it lands as a meteorite only if part survives.",
      "Lie on your back somewhere dark for half an hour and you will probably see a few shooting stars. Despite the name, they have nothing to do with stars. Each one is a piece of rock or dust, often no bigger than a grain of sand, hitting the atmosphere at roughly 11 to 72 kilometres per second. It heats the air so violently that it vaporises and makes the air glow, usually 70 to 100 kilometres up.\n\nAstronomers use three words. A meteoroid drifts in space. A meteor is the glowing streak. A meteorite is any piece that survives and lands on the ground."],
    ["Why showers return", "Meteor showers happen when Earth passes through trails of dust shed by comets, at the same point in its orbit each year.",
      "On most nights you might see a handful of random meteors an hour. On certain dates each year the count jumps, sometimes to dozens an hour. These are meteor showers, and they arrive like clockwork because Earth crosses the same streams of debris at the same point in its orbit. Most streams were shed by comets as they swung past the Sun.\n\nHalley's Comet, for example, feeds two showers: the Eta Aquariids in May and the Orionids in October. The meteors seem to fly out from one point, the radiant, and the shower is named after the constellation it sits in. It is a perspective effect, like parallel railway lines appearing to meet in the distance."],
    ["Showers for Australians", "From Australia, the Eta Aquariids in May and the Geminids in December are among the best showers to watch.",
      "Some famous showers favour the north. The Perseids in August are low or invisible from much of Australia. Southern observers do better with the Eta Aquariids, peaking in early May, whose radiant rises higher in our pre-dawn sky. The Geminids, peaking around the middle of December, are among the richest showers of the year. Unusually, they come from an asteroid called Phaethon rather than a comet.\n\nFor any shower, get away from city lights, let your eyes adjust for at least 20 minutes and lie back to take in as much sky as possible. Counts are usually best after midnight, when your side of Earth faces forward into the debris stream."],
    ["Rocks from the sky", "Most meteorites are stony, some preserve material older than the Sun, and very large impacts leave craters.",
      "Meteorites come in three families. Stony ones are the most common, and many are chondrites containing round grains formed in the dust cloud that built the planets about 4.6 billion years ago. Iron meteorites are metal chunks, thought to come from the cores of shattered asteroids. Stony-iron ones mix both.\n\nOn 28 September 1969 a meteorite broke up over Murchison in Victoria. It contains amino acids, the building blocks of proteins, and grains older than the Sun itself.\n\nVery large rocks barely notice the air and blast out craters, like the 880-metre Wolfe Creek Crater in Western Australia. In 2013 a roughly 20-metre object exploded over Chelyabinsk, Russia, and flying glass injured around 1,500 people."],
    ["Found a space rock?", "A real meteorite is usually unusually heavy, often magnetic and has a thin, dark, glassy crust, but most suspected finds are Earth rocks.",
      "Australia's deserts are excellent meteorite hunting grounds. Dark rocks stand out on pale ground, and the dry climate slows weathering. Curtin University's Desert Fireball Network uses cameras to photograph bright fireballs so their landing spots can be calculated and fragments recovered.\n\nIf you think you have found one, look for a rock that feels unusually heavy for its size, often attracts a magnet and has a thin, dark, glassy crust from melting during its fall. Real meteorites rarely have bubbly holes. Most candidates turn out to be iron-rich Earth rocks or industrial slag, nicknamed meteorwrongs. State museums can often identify specimens, and in some states meteorites legally belong to the state, so check the rules before selling one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which description fits a meteorite?", ["The glowing streak seen in the night sky","A space rock that survives and lands on Earth","A small rocky body drifting through space","The glowing tail of a comet near the Sun"], 1, "A meteorite is the part of a space rock that survives the fall and reaches the ground."],
    [1, "Why do meteor showers return on the same dates each year?", ["Comets cross the sky on a strict yearly timetable","The Moon sheds dust at the same point each year","Earth passes through the same debris trails each year","Old satellites all burn up together on set dates"], 2, "Showers repeat because Earth's orbit takes it through the same debris streams each year."],
    [2, "Why are the Eta Aquariids a better choice than the Perseids for Australian viewers?", ["They are caused by a much larger comet","They only occur in the middle of the day","They are brighter than any other shower","Their radiant rises higher in the pre-dawn southern sky"], 3, "A shower is only as good as its radiant is high, and the Perseids sit mostly in the northern sky."],
    [3, "What makes the Murchison meteorite scientifically valuable?", ["It contains amino acids and grains older than the Sun","It is made of pure iron and nickel","It formed from a broken piece of the Moon","It is the largest meteorite ever found"], 0, "Murchison preserves ancient material, including organic building blocks and pre-solar grains."],
    [4, "Which clue suggests a rock might be a real meteorite?", ["It floats in water and feels remarkably light","It is full of bubbly holes and gas pockets","It feels heavy and has a thin, dark, glassy crust","It crumbles easily into a chalky white powder"], 2, "Heavy weight and a melted dark crust are typical of real meteorites, while bubbly holes suggest slag."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Most shooting stars are smaller than a grain of rice. So what lands?"});

WB.L("neutron-stars-pulsars", "space", "advanced", "Neutron stars and pulsars",
  "Imagine squeezing more than the mass of the Sun into a ball the size of a city, then spinning it hundreds of times a second.",
  [
    ["City-sized and crushing", "A neutron star packs more mass than the Sun into a ball about 20 kilometres across, left behind when a massive star's core collapses.",
      "A neutron star is about 1.4 times the mass of the Sun, yet only around 20 kilometres across, roughly the size of a large city. A teaspoon of its material would weigh something like a billion tonnes on Earth. Its surface is thought to be incredibly smooth, with mountains only millimetres tall. Beneath it, matter is crushed so tightly that most electrons and protons have merged into neutrons.\n\nThe core of a massive star fuses elements until it makes iron, which cannot release energy by fusion. Gravity wins, and the core collapses in a fraction of a second. The outer layers rebound in a supernova. A lighter core becomes a neutron star; a heavier one becomes a black hole."],
    ["Little green men", "Pulsars are spinning neutron stars whose beams sweep past Earth like a lighthouse, and the first was spotted in 1967 by a postgraduate student.",
      "In 1967 Jocelyn Bell, a postgraduate student at Cambridge, noticed a bit of \"scruff\" on chart paper from a new radio telescope. It was a signal pulsing with astonishing regularity, about every 1.3 seconds. The team half jokingly labelled it LGM-1, for \"little green men\". More pulsing sources elsewhere made an alien explanation very unlikely. The 1974 Nobel Prize in Physics went to her supervisor Antony Hewish and Martin Ryle, an omission many scientists consider unfair.\n\nThe sources were rapidly spinning neutron stars. A collapsing core spins faster as it shrinks, like a skater pulling in their arms. Magnetic fields channel beams of radiation from the poles, and each time a beam crosses Earth, telescopes record a pulse. The fastest known spins over 700 times a second."],
    ["Extreme magnetars", "Magnetars are neutron stars with the strongest magnetic fields known, capable of colossal outbursts.",
      "Some neutron stars have magnetic fields far more extreme than usual. Magnetars are estimated to have fields up to around a thousand trillion times stronger than Earth's. As a magnetar's crust shifts under these enormous stresses, it can unleash sudden bursts of X-rays and gamma rays.\n\nIn December 2004 a giant flare from a magnetar called SGR 1806-20, on the far side of our galaxy, was so powerful that it measurably disturbed Earth's upper atmosphere. Astronomers now suspect that some magnetars are behind fast radio bursts, brief and extremely energetic flashes of radio waves detected from across the universe."],
    ["Collisions that forge gold", "When two neutron stars merge, they forge heavy elements such as gold and platinum.",
      "Many neutron stars live in pairs, spiralling slowly towards each other. In 1974 Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor found a binary pulsar whose orbit shrank exactly as Einstein's theory predicts if the pair radiates gravitational waves. It earned a Nobel Prize in 1993.\n\nOn 17 August 2017, detectors caught the gravitational waves of two neutron stars colliding about 130 million light-years away. Telescopes worldwide, including in Australia, watched the glowing aftermath, called a kilonova. Its light carried the signature of freshly made heavy elements. Astronomers estimate that mergers like this have produced a significant share of the universe's gold, platinum and other heavy metals, possibly including some of the gold in your jewellery."],
    ["Clocks across the galaxy", "Millisecond pulsars keep time almost as well as atomic clocks, so astronomers use them to test physics and detect gravitational waves.",
      "Some millisecond pulsars tick with a regularity that rivals atomic clocks. Australia has played a big role in studying them, and CSIRO's Parkes radio telescope, Murriyang, has discovered a large share of all known pulsars.\n\nBy timing dozens of pulsars over many years, astronomers watch for tiny shared shifts in when their pulses arrive. In 2023, several international teams, including the Parkes Pulsar Timing Array, reported evidence of a faint background of very long gravitational waves rippling through the galaxy, likely produced by pairs of supermassive black holes.\n\nPulsars have even been proposed as a galactic GPS. The plaques on the Pioneer probes and the Voyager records already carry a map of the Sun's position relative to 14 pulsars, a cosmic return address."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is left behind when a massive star's core collapses and the outer layers explode?", ["A white dwarf about the size of Earth","A neutron star or, if heavier, a black hole","A fresh star about the size of the Sun","A cloud of thin gas with no dense core"], 1, "The mass of the collapsing core decides whether a neutron star or a black hole remains."],
    [1, "Why do pulsars appear to pulse?", ["They swell and shrink every few seconds","Clouds of dust regularly pass in front of them","They switch on and off like a signal beacon","Beams from their poles sweep across Earth as they spin"], 3, "Pulsars shine steadily, but their tilted beams sweep past us like a lighthouse."],
    [2, "What sets magnetars apart from other neutron stars?", ["They have the strongest magnetic fields known","They are the largest neutron stars","They spin faster than any other star","They are made mostly of iron"], 0, "Magnetars have extreme magnetic fields that can trigger huge bursts of X-rays and gamma rays."],
    [3, "What did the 2017 neutron star merger help show?", ["Neutron stars are mostly made of iron","Mergers can forge heavy elements like gold","Gravity travels much slower than light","Pulsars are signals made by aliens"], 1, "The kilonova's light carried the signature of freshly made heavy elements."],
    [4, "Why are millisecond pulsars useful to astronomers?", ["They are bright enough to see by eye at night","They give out enough heat to warm planets","They keep time as precisely as cosmic clocks","They point astronomers towards alien life"], 2, "Their near-perfect timing lets astronomers spot tiny shifts such as passing gravitational waves."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Squeeze a star into a city, spin it hundreds of times a second. What then?"});

WB.L("gravitational-waves", "space", "advanced", "Gravitational waves: hearing the universe",
  "A century after Einstein predicted them, scientists caught a ripple in space itself that had travelled for over a billion years from two colliding black holes.",
  [
    ["Ripples too faint to feel", "Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime from accelerating masses, so faint that even colliding black holes barely disturb Earth.",
      "Drop a stone into a pond and ripples spread outward. In 1916 Albert Einstein realised his theory of general relativity predicted something similar in space itself. Mass and energy curve spacetime, and when massive objects accelerate, especially in close orbits, they send out ripples at the speed of light. A passing wave stretches space in one direction and squeezes it in the other, including the space you occupy.\n\nBut spacetime is extraordinarily stiff. The first wave measured changed the length of a four-kilometre detector arm by about a thousandth of the width of a proton. Einstein doubted anyone could ever measure it. Before then, the only evidence was indirect: a neutron star pair, found in 1974, spiralling together at just the rate expected."],
    ["The detectors", "LIGO detects the tiny stretching of space by watching for laser beams falling out of step along two four-kilometre arms.",
      "The Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, LIGO, has two detectors in the United States, at Hanford in Washington state and Livingston in Louisiana. Each is shaped like a giant L, with two arms four kilometres long.\n\nA laser beam is split in two and sent down both arms, bouncing off mirrors before the beams are recombined. Normally they cancel out perfectly. If a gravitational wave passes, one arm stretches while the other shrinks, the beams fall out of step and a flicker of light appears. Two widely separated detectors help rule out local disturbances such as trucks or earthquakes. Europe's Virgo and Japan's KAGRA have since joined, helping pinpoint sources in the sky."],
    ["The first chirp", "In September 2015 LIGO caught waves from two black holes merging about 1.3 billion years ago, a century after Einstein's prediction.",
      "On 14 September 2015, just days after the upgraded detectors switched on, both LIGO sites recorded the same signal, milliseconds apart. Turned into sound, it rose in pitch and volume and ended in a sudden chirp. It came from two black holes, each about 30 times the mass of the Sun, merging 1.3 billion years ago. In the final fraction of a second, roughly three Suns' worth of mass became gravitational waves, briefly outpowering all the stars in the observable universe combined.\n\nThe discovery was announced in February 2016, almost a century after Einstein's prediction. Rainer Weiss, Barry Barish and Kip Thorne won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics for it."],
    ["Australia's part", "Australian researchers helped build LIGO's technology and analyse its data, and now work together through OzGrav.",
      "Australia has been part of the gravitational wave story for decades. Researchers at the Australian National University, the University of Western Australia, the University of Adelaide and other institutions developed technologies used in LIGO, including precise laser and optics techniques and ways to reduce quantum noise in the measurements. Australians were among the authors of the discovery papers.\n\nToday many of these researchers belong to OzGrav, the ARC Centre of Excellence for Gravitational Wave Discovery, which works on detecting signals, analysing data and following up events with telescopes. Australia's Southern Hemisphere position helps, since it covers parts of the sky that northern telescopes cannot see well."],
    ["Light and gravity together", "A 2017 neutron star merger was seen in both gravitational waves and light, confirming gravity travels at the speed of light.",
      "Black hole mergers produce gravitational waves but little or no light. Neutron star collisions are different. On 17 August 2017, LIGO and Virgo picked up a merger about 130 million light-years away. About 1.7 seconds later, a space telescope recorded a burst of gamma rays from the same region, and within hours telescopes worldwide were watching the debris. This was the birth of multi-messenger astronomy: studying one event through different signals.\n\nThe near-simultaneous arrival of light and gravitational waves after 130 million years confirmed that gravity travels at the speed of light to extraordinary precision, and ruled out several alternative theories of gravity.\n\nDetections are now routine, with hundreds of candidates so far. Future instruments, such as ESA's planned LISA, will listen for slower waves."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is it so hard to detect gravitational waves?", ["Spacetime is so stiff that effects are tiny","They travel far more slowly than light","They can only be produced inside black holes","They are absorbed by Earth's atmosphere"], 0, "Only the most violent events make waves, and by arrival they stretch a four-kilometre arm by less than a proton's width."],
    [1, "How does LIGO detect a gravitational wave?", ["By watching distant stars wobble in the sky","By listening with microphones in orbit","By weighing distant objects very precisely","By spotting laser beams that fall out of step"], 3, "A passing wave stretches one arm and squeezes the other, so recombined laser beams no longer cancel."],
    [2, "What produced the first gravitational waves ever detected, in 2015?", ["A supernova in our own galaxy","Two black holes merging","The Big Bang itself","The Sun's magnetic field"], 1, "The signal came from two roughly 30-solar-mass black holes spiralling together and merging."],
    [3, "What is OzGrav?", ["An Australian detector built in the outback","A satellite launched by an Australian agency","An Australian gravitational wave research centre","A NASA mission to search for black holes"], 2, "OzGrav brings Australian researchers together to detect and analyse gravitational waves."],
    [4, "What did the 2017 neutron star merger confirm?", ["Black holes give off bright light","Gravitational waves are too weak to detect","Neutron stars are extremely rare","Gravity travels at the speed of light"], 3, "Gravitational waves and gamma rays arrived about 1.7 seconds apart after 130 million years."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A ripple in space itself crossed 1.3 billion years to reach us. What made it?"});

WB.L("ocean-moons", "space", "intermediate", "Ocean moons: hidden seas of the outer solar system",
  "Some of the best places to look for alien life may be dark oceans hidden beneath the ice of moons orbiting Jupiter and Saturn.",
  [
    ["Hidden seas, tidal heat", "Several icy moons hide liquid oceans, kept from freezing by tidal heating as their planet's gravity flexes them.",
      "Out past Mars, sunlight is weak and the surface of Jupiter's and Saturn's moons sits well below minus 100 degrees Celsius. Yet spacecraft show that several of these moons appear to hide global oceans of liquid water beneath shells of ice. Europa may hold more water than all of Earth's oceans combined.\n\nHow does water stay liquid? Mainly through tidal heating. These moons follow slightly oval orbits around giant planets, so the pull of gravity changes and squeezes and stretches them. Like a paperclip bent back and forth, they warm by friction. Jupiter's Io is the extreme case, the most volcanically active world in the solar system. Gentler heating, plus heat from radioactive elements in the rock, appears enough to keep oceans liquid under kilometres of ice."],
    ["Europa's hidden ocean", "Europa's cracked, young ice shell covers a salty ocean that probably touches a rocky seafloor where chemistry could support life.",
      "Europa is slightly smaller than our Moon, but its surface is bright white ice crisscrossed by reddish brown cracks, with very few craters. That means the surface is young and constantly reshaped.\n\nIn the late 1990s NASA's Galileo spacecraft measured how Europa disturbed Jupiter's magnetic field, and the best explanation was a salty, electrically conducting ocean under the ice. The ice shell is thought to be several to a few dozen kilometres thick, over an ocean perhaps around 100 kilometres deep.\n\nCrucially, that ocean probably sits on a rocky seafloor. Water and rock could react chemically there, as they do around hydrothermal vents in Earth's deep oceans, where life thrives without sunlight."],
    ["Enceladus geysers", "Saturn's tiny moon Enceladus sprays its ocean into space, and Cassini flew through the plumes and sampled them.",
      "Enceladus is only about 500 kilometres across, small enough to fit within the width of Victoria, so scientists expected little. Then in 2005 NASA's Cassini spacecraft spotted jets of water vapour and ice grains erupting from long cracks near its south pole, nicknamed tiger stripes. The plumes feed one of Saturn's rings.\n\nCassini later flew straight through them, essentially tasting an alien ocean. It found water, salts, carbon dioxide, ammonia and organic molecules. Tiny silica grains hinted at hot water reacting with rock on the seafloor, and later analysis found phosphorus, an essential ingredient for life on Earth. Enceladus appears to tick many boxes for habitability: liquid water, chemical energy and the right raw materials."],
    ["Titan and Ganymede", "Titan has methane lakes over a likely water ocean, while Ganymede, the largest moon, probably hides a salty ocean too.",
      "Saturn's Titan is bigger than Mercury and the only moon with a thick atmosphere, mostly nitrogen. At around minus 180 degrees Celsius, water is rock-hard, but methane and ethane flow as liquids. Titan has clouds, rain, rivers and seas of liquid hydrocarbons. In 2005 Europe's Huygens probe parachuted through the orange haze and landed on a damp plain strewn with rounded ice pebbles. Measurements suggest a water ocean lies deep below.\n\nJupiter's Ganymede is the largest moon in the solar system and the only one known to generate its own magnetic field. Hubble observations of its shifting auroras suggest a salty ocean beneath the surface. Callisto may have one too, and oceans are suspected further out, including Triton and Pluto."],
    ["Missions and caution", "New missions are heading to these moons, and old spacecraft were deliberately destroyed to avoid contaminating them.",
      "If buried oceans are common, liquid water may be far more widespread in the universe than on sunlit rocky planets like Earth, greatly increasing the number of places life could arise.\n\nSeveral spacecraft are on their way. ESA's JUICE, launched in 2023, will study Jupiter's icy moons and eventually orbit Ganymede. NASA's Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, will make dozens of close flybys of Europa. NASA's Dragonfly, a nuclear-powered rotorcraft, is planned to fly between sites on Titan in the 2030s.\n\nScientists take great care not to contaminate these worlds with Earth microbes, a principle called planetary protection. That is why Galileo was deliberately plunged into Jupiter in 2003, and Cassini into Saturn in 2017, as their fuel ran low."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What mainly keeps these moons' oceans from freezing?", ["Strong sunlight warming their frozen surfaces","Thick greenhouse atmospheres trapping heat","Heat from tidal flexing by their planet's gravity","Warmth radiated from the giant planet's surface"], 2, "Tidal flexing generates heat inside the moon, helped by radioactive heat from rock."],
    [1, "Why is Europa's ocean especially interesting for life?", ["Its water is warm enough to be boiling","It probably meets rock where chemistry can happen","It is exposed directly to the Sun's light","It is made mostly of methane and ethane"], 1, "Water reacting with rock could give chemical energy, as at Earth's hydrothermal vents."],
    [2, "What did Cassini discover at Enceladus?", ["A thick nitrogen atmosphere like Titan's","Active lava volcanoes like those on Io","Rings of its own that surround the moon","Jets of water and ice near its south pole"], 3, "Cassini sampled the plumes and found water, salts and organic molecules."],
    [3, "What are Titan's lakes and seas made of?", ["Liquid methane and ethane","Liquid water and salts","Molten rock and metal","Liquid carbon dioxide"], 0, "At around minus 180 degrees Celsius water is frozen solid, but methane and ethane can flow."],
    [4, "Why was Cassini deliberately sent into Saturn in 2017?", ["To measure the composition of Saturn's core","To clear the orbit for a newer spacecraft","To avoid contaminating moons that might host life","Because debris had damaged its instruments"], 2, "Planetary protection means avoiding Earth microbes reaching worlds that could be habitable."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Some of the best places to look for alien life are dark seas under ice."});

WB.L("space-junk", "space", "beginner", "Space junk: the litter problem in orbit",
  "Tens of thousands of dead satellites and rocket parts are racing around Earth faster than bullets, and once in 1979 a big piece landed near Esperance.",
  [
    ["A fast-moving cloud", "Earth is ringed by dead satellites, rocket stages and fragments travelling so fast that even tiny pieces cause serious damage.",
      "Since Sputnik in 1957, much of what has gone up has stayed there. Space junk includes dead satellites, used rocket stages, lost tools, flakes of paint and fragments from explosions and collisions. Agencies track tens of thousands of pieces larger than about a softball, and estimates suggest around a million pieces bigger than a centimetre, plus many millions smaller. Most sits in low Earth orbit, the busy region used by the International Space Station.\n\nTo stay up there, an object travels at about 28,000 kilometres per hour, many times faster than a rifle bullet. At that speed even a fleck of paint carries surprising energy, and Space Shuttle windows were replaced after being chipped. The ISS has extra shielding and regularly fires its engines to dodge tracked debris."],
    ["Collisions and tests", "Accidental collisions and deliberate missile tests create debris clouds, raising the risk of a runaway chain reaction known as Kessler syndrome.",
      "On 10 February 2009 a working Iridium satellite hit a dead Russian satellite, Kosmos 2251, about 790 kilometres above Siberia. It was the first known accidental collision between two intact satellites and created well over a thousand trackable fragments.\n\nIn 1978 NASA scientist Donald Kessler warned that if debris became dense enough, collisions would create more fragments, causing more collisions in a cascade. This is the Kessler syndrome, and in its worst form some orbits could become too risky to use for generations.\n\nSome clouds were made on purpose. In 2007 China destroyed its own weather satellite with a missile, creating over 3,000 trackable fragments, the largest single event on record. In 2021 Russia did the same, forcing ISS crew to shelter. Since 2022 several countries, including Australia, have pledged not to hold such tests."],
    ["What goes up", "Most debris eventually sinks and burns up, but large objects sometimes reach the ground, including in Australia.",
      "Thin traces of atmosphere slowly drag on objects in low orbit, so they sink and eventually burn up on re-entry. Large, sturdy parts can survive.\n\nIn July 1979 the American space station Skylab broke apart over the Indian Ocean and scattered debris around Esperance in Western Australia. Nobody was hurt, and the local shire famously fined NASA $400 for littering. An American radio host eventually paid it decades later.\n\nIn 2022, pieces of a SpaceX capsule's trunk landed on farms in the Snowy Mountains of New South Wales. Today operators often steer big spacecraft down over a remote part of the South Pacific near Point Nemo, the place furthest from land, nicknamed the spacecraft cemetery."],
    ["Crowded skies", "Thousands of new internet satellites bring faster connections but make orbit busier and astronomy harder.",
      "The number of active satellites has soared, mainly because of megaconstellations: huge fleets of small satellites providing internet from low orbit. SpaceX's Starlink alone has launched many thousands, and others are building their own. These networks bring fast internet to remote areas, including outback Australia, and are designed to fall out of orbit within a few years once retired.\n\nBut more satellites mean more close approaches, and operators must perform large numbers of avoidance manoeuvres. Astronomers have raised another concern: sunlit satellites leave bright streaks across telescope images and can interfere with radio observations. Darker coatings and sunshades have been tried, but the growing traffic is changing how scientists plan their observations."],
    ["Cleaning up", "The first fix is not to make more mess: rules now push operators to remove satellites after use, while capture technologies are tested.",
      "International guidelines have long asked operators to remove satellites from busy low orbits within 25 years of the end of a mission. In 2022 the United States regulator set a stricter five-year rule for satellites it licenses. Rocket stages are increasingly designed to burn up quickly, and satellites carry fuel to lower themselves at the end of their lives.\n\nRemoving existing junk is harder, because every piece is moving fast and tumbling. Japan's Astroscale has flown missions that approached and inspected a discarded rocket stage, and European projects are developing robotic arms and nets to catch dead satellites. In Australia, the Australian Space Agency works with industry on space situational awareness, tracking objects to help keep orbits safe."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can even a tiny fleck of paint damage a spacecraft in low orbit?", ["It is made of very dense metal","It is hot from sunlight","It is magnetic and sticks to hulls","It travels at about 28,000 kilometres per hour"], 3, "At orbital speed, even tiny pieces carry a great deal of energy."],
    [1, "What is the Kessler syndrome?", ["An illness astronauts can develop in orbit","A chain reaction of collisions","A rule for retiring satellites","A type of satellite engine"], 1, "Each collision makes more fragments, which cause more collisions, and some orbits could become unusable."],
    [2, "What usually happens to debris in low orbit over time?", ["It drifts out into deep space","It stays in the same orbit forever","Drag slowly pulls it down so it burns up","It is collected by passing satellites"], 2, "Thin traces of atmosphere drag on objects in low orbit until they re-enter."],
    [3, "Why are astronomers concerned about megaconstellations?", ["They block sunlight from reaching Earth","Sunlit satellites streak telescope images","They warm the upper atmosphere","They change the Moon's orbit"], 1, "Reflected sunlight from many satellites interferes with astronomical observations."],
    [4, "What is the first and simplest fix for the space junk problem?", ["Retiring satellites promptly after use","Firing missiles at the debris","Moving every satellite to a higher orbit","Pausing all rocket launches"], 0, "Prevention, such as retiring satellites promptly, is easier than removing tumbling junk."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A rubbish fine sent to NASA from a small Australian shire. What was dumped?"});

WB.L("cosmic-distance-ladder", "space", "intermediate", "How we measure the universe",
  "No one has ever stretched a tape measure to a star, yet we know distances across billions of light-years. The trick is a ladder of clever steps.",
  [
    ["How far is far?", "Astronomers measure space in light-years, the distance light travels in a year, using a chain of methods called the cosmic distance ladder.",
      "Light covers about 300,000 kilometres every second. Even so, sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach us, and light from Proxima Centauri, the nearest star beyond the Sun, takes over four years. So astronomers use the light-year: the distance light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion kilometres. It is a distance, not a time.\n\nThat has a fascinating consequence. Look at a star a thousand light-years away and you see light that left it a thousand years ago. Looking further means looking back in time.\n\nNo single method works for everything. Astronomers built a chain of techniques, each checked against the last, called the cosmic distance ladder."],
    ["Echoes and parallax", "Nearby distances come from timing radar and laser echoes, and nearby stars from parallax, their apparent shift as Earth orbits the Sun.",
      "The first rung is our own neighbourhood. Knowing the speed of light, we can bounce signals off objects and time the echo. Radar off Venus, Mars and asteroids fixes the solar system's scale; the Earth to Sun distance, the astronomical unit, is about 150 million kilometres. Lasers fired at reflectors left by Apollo astronauts give the Moon's distance to within millimetres, and show it drifting away a few centimetres a year.\n\nNext comes parallax. Hold a thumb at arm's length and look with one eye, then the other: it seems to jump against the background. Astronomers use two positions of Earth, six months apart. A nearby star shifts slightly against distant ones, and geometry turns that shift into a distance. Friedrich Bessel first managed it in 1838; the Gaia spacecraft has since measured over a billion stars."],
    ["Leavitt and Hubble", "Henrietta Leavitt's discovery that a Cepheid's pulse rate reveals its brightness let Hubble show that Andromeda is a separate galaxy.",
      "Parallax only works nearby. Beyond it, astronomers need standard candles: objects of known true brightness.\n\nIn the early 1900s Henrietta Swan Leavitt of Harvard Observatory studied the Small Magellanic Cloud, visible from Australia. She found that Cepheid variables, stars that brighten and dim rhythmically, follow a pattern: the slower the pulse, the brighter the star. So a Cepheid's rhythm reveals its distance.\n\nIn the 1920s Edwin Hubble used this to settle whether spiral nebulae lay inside the Milky Way. Cepheids in Andromeda showed it was a separate galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away. The known universe grew from one galaxy to billions."],
    ["Exploding yardsticks", "Type Ia supernovae are bright enough to measure distances across billions of light-years and revealed that the universe's expansion is speeding up.",
      "Cepheids are not bright enough to see in very distant galaxies. For those, astronomers use Type Ia supernovae. These are thought to occur when a white dwarf, the dense remnant of a star like the Sun, is pushed over a critical limit and blows apart.\n\nBecause they explode in a fairly consistent way, their peak brightness can be worked out. That makes them standard candles visible across billions of light-years.\n\nIn 1998 two teams used them to discover that the universe's expansion is speeding up, not slowing down. One team was led by Brian Schmidt, working at the Australian National University's Mount Stromlo Observatory near Canberra. He shared the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics with Saul Perlmutter and Adam Riess."],
    ["Redshift and a puzzle", "Redshift reveals the distance of the most remote galaxies, but two ways of measuring the expansion rate disagree.",
      "At the top of the ladder is redshift. As the universe expands, light is stretched to longer, redder wavelengths. The further away a galaxy is, the faster it recedes and the redder its light, a relationship known as Hubble's law. Once the ladder is calibrated, redshift tells astronomers roughly how far away a galaxy is.\n\nBut there is a puzzle. Measuring today's expansion rate with the ladder of Cepheids and supernovae gives a noticeably higher answer than calculating it from the faint afterglow of the Big Bang, the cosmic microwave background. This mismatch, called the Hubble tension, has persisted as measurements have improved. It might be a subtle error somewhere, or a sign that our picture of the universe is missing something."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does it mean to see a star that is a thousand light-years away?", ["You see it as it is right now","You see light that left it a thousand years ago","You see it with a thousand times its true brightness","You see only its reflected sunlight"], 1, "Light takes time to travel, so looking further away means looking further back in time."],
    [1, "How does parallax measure the distance to a nearby star?", ["By timing a radar echo off the star","By analysing the colour of its light alone","By measuring how quickly it pulses","By its apparent shift as Earth orbits the Sun"], 3, "Viewing from opposite sides of Earth's orbit makes nearby stars shift against distant ones, and geometry gives the distance."],
    [2, "What did Henrietta Leavitt discover about Cepheid variables?", ["The slower they pulse, the brighter they truly are","They are all the same distance from Earth","They are planets that orbit distant stars","They appear only in the Southern Hemisphere"], 0, "A Cepheid's pulse rate reveals its true brightness, and comparing that with its apparent dimness gives distance."],
    [3, "Why are Type Ia supernovae useful for measuring distance?", ["They stay bright for many centuries","Their peak brightness is consistent and known","They are the closest stars to our planet","They always occur inside our own galaxy"], 1, "A known brightness lets astronomers work out distance from how dim the explosion looks."],
    [4, "What is the Hubble tension?", ["Redshift having no link to distance at all","Cepheids failing to pulse at a regular rate","Two methods giving different expansion rates","The universe slowing down faster than predicted"], 2, "Two good methods give different expansion rates, which might point to an error or to missing physics."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Nobody has stretched a tape measure to a star. So how do we know how far?"});

WB.L("first-nations-astronomy", "space", "beginner", "First Nations astronomy",
  "Long before telescopes, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples read the night sky as a calendar, a map, a law book and a library of stories.",
  [
    ["The oldest sky watchers", "Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have read the night sky for many thousands of years, with knowledge that differs between nations.",
      "Australia's First Peoples have lived on this continent for at least 50,000 years, and many researchers think longer. For all that time they have looked up at the same southern sky, and their knowledge of it is woven through culture, law and daily life.\n\nThere is no single \"Aboriginal astronomy\". Australia has hundreds of distinct nations and language groups, each with its own stories and ways of reading the sky, though some stories are shared across vast distances. The stars were used to tell the seasons, find food, navigate, mark ceremonies and pass on rules for living. Some of this knowledge is sacred and not for public sharing."],
    ["The Emu in the Sky", "Many groups see an emu in the dark dust lanes of the Milky Way, and its changing position tracks the emu breeding season.",
      "Western constellations join up bright stars. Many Aboriginal traditions also look at the spaces between them. The best known example is the Emu in the Sky. Its head is the Coalsack, a dark dust cloud beside the Southern Cross, and its neck, body and legs stretch along dark lanes in the Milky Way.\n\nOn autumn evenings, after dark, the emu appears to be running across the sky. For a number of groups this coincides with the time emus are mating and laying eggs, a signal for when eggs could be collected. Later in the year the emu's shape appears to change position, reflecting different stages of the breeding cycle."],
    ["The Seven Sisters", "The Pleiades star cluster is the heart of the Seven Sisters story, which links sites across thousands of kilometres of desert.",
      "The Pleiades, a small, tight cluster of stars, has inspired stories in cultures from ancient Greece to Japan. In Australia it is the heart of the Seven Sisters, one of the most widespread and important Aboriginal stories.\n\nIn many desert versions, sisters are pursued across the country by a man, often linked to stars in the constellation Westerners call Orion. As they flee, their journey creates and names features of the landscape, forming songlines: routes that link sites over thousands of kilometres and carry knowledge, law and ceremony.\n\nIn 2017 the National Museum of Australia staged Songlines: Tracking the Seven Sisters, developed with Anangu, Martu and Ngaanyatjarra communities and sharing parts of the story through paintings, song and dance."],
    ["Tagai and star calendars", "Star figures such as Tagai, and the seasonal appearance of particular stars, guide when to plant, fish, hunt and expect weather changes.",
      "For Torres Strait Islanders, the most important sky figure is Tagai, a great fisherman standing in his canoe. In common descriptions, his left hand holds a fishing spear, the Southern Cross, and his right hand holds a sorbi fruit, the stars of Corvus. His canoe and crew are made of other stars. Tagai teaches the importance of following rules and sharing, and his position guides when to plant, fish and hunt turtle and dugong.\n\nAcross Australia, stars appearing at dawn or dusk signal events on the ground. In the 1850s William Stanbridge recorded Boorong knowledge in north-western Victoria, including a star linked to when malleefowl build nesting mounds. These calendars are tailored to local country, since seasons differ across Australia."],
    ["Tides, eclipses and respect", "First Nations sky knowledge includes careful observations of tides, eclipses and variable stars, and it belongs to specific communities.",
      "Sky knowledge was not only stories; it recorded careful observations. The Yolngu of north-east Arnhem Land link the tides to the Moon, describing the Moon filling with water as tides rise, which captures a real connection. Several groups have stories explaining eclipses, often as the Sun woman and Moon man coming together. Researchers have found traditions that appear to describe changing brightness in stars such as Betelgeuse and Antares, which astronomy recognises as variable. Some scholars think stories about Wolfe Creek Crater may preserve memories of real events, though that is hard to prove.\n\nTo learn more, seek sources made by or with communities, such as Aboriginal-run tours and museum resources. Stories differ between nations, and some knowledge is restricted."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is there no single 'Aboriginal astronomy'?", ["The sky looks different in each state","Hundreds of nations have their own sky stories","Most of the knowledge has been lost","Only a few groups ever watched the stars"], 1, "Knowledge is held by specific nations, so stories from one group should not be assumed to apply everywhere."],
    [1, "What forms the Emu in the Sky?", ["The brightest stars of Orion","The planets along the ecliptic","The dark dust lanes of the Milky Way","The Magellanic Clouds"], 2, "Many traditions read the dark spaces between stars, with the Coalsack forming the emu's head."],
    [2, "What do songlines in the Seven Sisters story do?", ["Predict when comets will return","Link sites and carry law and knowledge","Record the exact dates of eclipses","Measure the distances between stars"], 1, "The sisters' journey creates and names landscape features that connect country across thousands of kilometres."],
    [3, "How did many communities use the appearance of particular stars?", ["To measure how far away stars were","To predict earthquakes and cyclones","To calculate the size of the Moon","As seasonal calendars for food and weather"], 3, "Stars at dawn or dusk signalled events on country, so calendars are tailored to local seasons."],
    [4, "What do the Yolngu understand about tides?", ["They are caused by the wind","They are linked to the Moon","They happen only at night","They are caused by whales"], 1, "Yolngu knowledge connects the Moon to the tides, matching the real cause."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Long before telescopes, the dark gaps between stars told people when to hunt."});

